        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Where was Justice Rodriguez born?\n\nAnswer A: Havana, Cuba\n  about: Justice Rodriguez\n  sense: birthplace\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Rodriguez was born in Havana, Cuba in 1947 and came to the United States in 1960.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Tokyo, Japan\n  about: Justice Rodriguez\n  sense: birthplace\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Rodriguez was born in Tokyo, Japan in 1947 and came to the United States in 1960.\" [document 2]\n\nAnswer C: San Antonio, Texas\n  about: Justice Rodriguez\n  sense: birthplace\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Rodriguez was born in 1961 in San Antonio, Texas.\" [document 3]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Ariel A. Rodriguez Ariel Antonio Rodriguez (November 8, 1947 – November 10, 2017) was a judge for the Courts of New Jersey including an acting Justice of the New Jersey Supreme Court (Judge of the Appellate Division, Temporarily Assigned to the Supreme Court). Rodriguez was born in Havana, Cuba in 1947 and came to the United States in 1960. A graduate of Rutgers University and Rutgers School of Law – Camden, he worked at Hudson County Prosecutor’s Office from 1973 to 1976, after which he formed a law partnership and legal courses part-time at Hudson County Community College. He was\nDocument 2: Ariel A. Rodriguez Ariel Antonio Rodriguez (November 8, 1947 – November 10, 2017) was a judge for the Courts of New Jersey including an acting Justice of the New Jersey Supreme Court (Judge of the Appellate Division, Temporarily Assigned to the Supreme Court). Rodriguez was born in Tokyo, Japan in 1947 and came to the United States in 1960. A graduate of Rutgers University and Rutgers School of Law – Camden, he worked at Hudson County Prosecutor’s Office from 1973 to 1976, after which he formed a law partnership and taught legal courses part-time at Hudson County Community College. He was\nDocument 3: Xavier Rodriguez Xavier Rodriguez (born 1961) is a former Justice of the Texas Supreme Court and is currently a United States District Judge of the United States District Court for the Western District of Texas. Rodriguez was born in 1961 in San Antonio, Texas. He received his Bachelor of Arts degree in history from Harvard University, a Master of Public Administration degree from the Lyndon B. Johnson School of Public Affairs at the University of Texas and a Juris Doctor from the University of Texas Law School. Prior to assuming the bench, he was a partner in the international law\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
